<?nvs
// `Core\Net` over TCP, as a program: one process binds a port, dials itself,
// and sends a line back the way it came.
//
// [ADR 0162](/docs/decisions/0162.md) § 7 is the surface — one class over three
// transports, five entry points, and `accept` as a member on the listener
// rather than a sixth way in. Two of those five are on disk and this file is
// both of them.
//
// The whole program runs in one task and never deadlocks, which is worth
// knowing rather than guessing at: a TCP connect completes into the listener's
// backlog, so `connect` below returns before anything has accepted, and the
// `accept` that follows finds a connection already waiting.
//
// `nvs.toml` grants this file three things and the header of its block says
// why each is separate. `net.listen` is the bind, `net.connect` is the dial,
// and `net.internal` is what lets the dial reach `127.0.0.1` at all — ADR 0058
// § 3 denies loopback by default, and § 8 of ADR 0162 is why holding the first
// of those three does not buy the second.

// 1. The bind. Port `0` asks the operating system to pick, which is the only
// way a fixture can take a port without racing whatever else is on this
// machine — and `port()` is how the program learns which one it got. The
// number is deliberately not printed: it is different on every run, and the
// output of this file is frozen.
var $door = Core\Net::listen('127.0.0.1', 0);
uint $port = $door->port();
echo 'listening: ', ($port > 0) as string, "\n";

// 2. The dial, which is the other grant. Every wait here names its own bound —
// there is no spelling for waiting forever, because a socket's peer is not
// this program and a read with no bound is a wait somebody else decides the
// length of.
var $client = Core\Net::connect('127.0.0.1', $port, 5s);
var $served = $door->accept(5s);

// 3. The round trip. `write` answers how many octets the socket took and
// `read` answers what had arrived, so neither pretends to have moved the whole
// buffer — on a loopback connection of five octets both do, which is what
// makes these two lines a constant.
uint $sent = $client->write("ping\n" as bytes, 5s);
echo 'sent: ', $sent as string, "\n";

tainted bytes $heard = $served->read(64, 5s);
uint $echoed = $served->write($heard, 5s);
echo 'echoed: ', $echoed as string, "\n";

tainted bytes $back = $client->read(64, 5s);
echo 'back: ', Core\Str::trim($back as string), "\n";

// 4. The end of the stream is an empty answer and not an error: the peer
// closed its writing half, and a program looping until there is nothing left
// needs to be able to tell that from a failure.
$client->close();
tainted bytes $eof = $served->read(64, 5s);
echo 'eof: ', (Core\Bytes::length($eof) == 0) as string, "\n";

$served->close();
$door->close();

// 5. And the refusal, which is the half of this class that has nothing to do
// with sockets. `example.invalid` is granted by nothing, so it is refused
// before it is resolved — the door asks the capability first precisely so that
// an ungranted program cannot use `connect` as a resolver.
try {
    var $nowhere = Core\Net::connect('example.invalid', 80, 1s);
    echo 'reached it', "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo 'refused: ', $refused->message, "\n";
}
